WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.755
Strong reflection is grounded in evidence.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:13.925
Evidence can include facts, timelines, artifacts, logs, metrics, user outcomes, test results, peer feedback, customer behavior, incident records, or observed performance.

3
00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:19.286
Without evidence, reflection can become a debate between feelings and assumptions.

4
00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:24.796
Evidence does not remove judgment, but it gives judgment something firmer to work with.

5
00:00:24.796 --> 00:00:29.040
Separating facts from interpretations is a core reflective skill.

6
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.519
A fact might be that a deployment took forty minutes, three alerts fired, or a user abandoned a workflow.

7
00:00:35.519 --> 00:00:42.965
An interpretation might be that the release process is broken, the alerting is too sensitive, or the user was confused.

8
00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:49.890
Interpretations may be correct, but they should be tested against the evidence before becoming conclusions.

9
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.975
Feedback improves calibration.

10
00:00:51.975 --> 00:00:57.634
Calibration means matching confidence to actual performance, accuracy, or likelihood.

11
00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:07.016
A person may be overconfident because a past decision worked out, or underconfident because one visible mistake overshadowed many correct choices.

12
00:01:07.016 --> 00:01:15.654
Feedback from peers, users, mentors, systems, metrics, and outcomes helps people adjust confidence without overreacting to one event.

13
00:01:15.654 --> 00:01:23.696
Hindsight bias can distort reflection by making outcomes seem more predictable after they happen than they really were before.

14
00:01:23.696 --> 00:01:28.983
A decision can look obvious in review even if the evidence at the time was incomplete.

15
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:41.716
Good reflection asks what was knowable then, what signals were available, what uncertainty existed, and how future decisions can be better supported without pretending the past was simpler than it was.
